<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ehrenbreitstein Fortress</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ehrenbreitstein Fortress stands on a rock above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a> and the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. A castle there belonged to the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> from the 11th century and became a stronghold and residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>. Blown up by the French in 1801, it was rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> between 1817 and 1828 as one of the largest fortresses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ehrenbreitstein-fortress.jpg" alt="Ehrenbreitstein Fortress" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fritz Geller-Grimm · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Festung_Ehrenbreitstein_Luftbild_080.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
